# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING A PATTERN
#
# ANCHOR IT. A pattern without a leading slash matches a directory of that
# name at ANY DEPTH, including inside source trees.
#
# This has cost this project twice, eleven months apart, the same way both
# times:
#
#   vendor/  swallowed services/market-data/internal/vendor — the market-data
#            adapter, not vendored dependencies. A fresh clone could not build
#            that service.
#   build/   swallowed services/web/src/app/api/build — the route that reports
#            which commit the running bundle is. The endpoint written to
#            detect a stale deploy was itself missing from the deploy.
#
# The second happened AFTER the first was fixed and explained in this file,
# because the lesson was applied to one pattern and not to the three sitting
# beside it. Comments do not stop it, so two things now do:
#
#   .githooks/pre-commit        refuses the commit  (git config core.hooksPath .githooks)
#   repo-complete-verify        fails the sweep
#
# Both call infra/verify/lib/ignored-sources.mjs, which is the one definition
# of the rule: NOTHING under services/*/{src,internal,cmd,app} or
# packages/*/{src,internal,cmd,app} may be ignored. If something there really
# is generated, move it out of the source tree — do not write a negation. An
# exception to this rule is the next instance of this bug.
#
# WHY NOTHING ELSE CATCHES IT: `git add -A` says nothing, the commit succeeds,
# and an untracked file has no place in the commit it would be compared
# against. There is nothing to differ. It shows up as a missing file on a
# server, days later.
# --- Dependencies ---
node_modules/
# GO DEPENDENCY VENDORING, AT MODULE ROOTS ONLY.
#
# This was a bare `vendor/` and it matched any directory of that name at any
# depth — including services/market-data/internal/vendor, which is not vendored
# dependencies at all but this project's own market-data vendor adapter. Its one
# file was therefore excluded from the repository for as long as it existed, a
# fresh clone could not build market-data, and nothing noticed: an untracked file
# cannot fail a comparison against the commit it has no place in.
#
# Anchored and scoped so it still ignores what it was written for.
/vendor/
/packages/*/vendor/
/services/*/vendor/

# --- Build output ---
# ANCHORED, FOR THE REASON WRITTEN ABOVE `vendor/` — and these were left
# unanchored after that lesson was learned for one pattern and not the rest.
#
# `build/` matched any directory of that name at any depth, so
# services/web/src/app/api/build — a Next route reporting which commit the
# running bundle is — was silently excluded. It existed locally, compiled
# locally, passed typecheck locally, and was never committed. `git add -A`
# said nothing, the commit succeeded, and the route simply did not exist on
# the server: the endpoint written to detect a stale deploy was itself missing
# from the deploy. An untracked file cannot fail a comparison against the
# commit it has no place in.
#
# Build output lands in known places. These say those places.
/dist/
/packages/*/dist/
/services/*/dist/
/build/
/services/*/build/
*.tsbuildinfo
/coverage/
/packages/*/coverage/
/services/*/coverage/

# --- Go ---
/bin/
/packages/*/bin/
/services/*/bin/
*.exe
*.test
*.out

# --- Environment ---
.env
.env.*
# The TEMPLATES go in; only the filled-in files stay out. Key names are not
# secrets, and a machine nobody can configure is the same class of problem as a
# repository that is not the whole record of what runs. Without the second
# negation, .env.auth.example was silently ignored by the .env.* above — which
# is how three of these came to be missing in the first place.
!.env.example
!.env.*.example

# --- IDE / OS ---
# .idea/ and .vscode/ stay unanchored: they are named by tools, nobody writes
# source into them, and ignored-sources.mjs exempts them by name.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Logs & temp ---
*.log
# Anchored for the reason at the top. This was a bare `tmp/`, the third pattern
# of the same shape, still sitting here after vendor/ and build/ were fixed. It
# had not swallowed anything yet; planting services/web/src/app/tmp/page.tsx
# while proving the pre-commit hook is how it was found.
/tmp/
/packages/*/tmp/
/services/*/tmp/

# --- Local tooling (never pushed to remote / VPS) ---
/.commandcode/
/.claude/
# The working architecture reference. It lives on the owner's machine only;
# the documents under docs/ that cite its sections are the public record.
/architecture.md

# Build artefacts produced on the host by infra/systemd/install.sh.
# Untracked binaries made every deployed-version check report a dirty working
# tree, which would have trained everyone to ignore the one signal that says
# production is running code nobody reviewed.
/scheduler-bin/
services/*/server
services/*/backfill
# The lease-race binary guard-verify builds from cmd/leaserace and removes again.
# Listed so that a run which dies before its cleanup cannot make
# repo-complete-verify report an unclassified file.
.guard-verify-race

# go.work.sum WAS IN THE LIST ABOVE, and did not belong there. It is not a build
# artefact: it is the checksum record for the workspace, and go.work itself is
# committed. "The toolchain can regenerate it" is not the same claim as "the
# toolchain will regenerate the same thing" — what it writes depends on which
# module versions it happens to need at the time. If go.work is part of the
# record of what builds here, its sums are part of the same record.

# The build stamp lives inside dist/ and is written by the installer.
# Listed so a future dist/ ignore rule cannot silently take it with it.
!services/*/dist/.build-commit
/.dust-verify-build/

# Verbose go test logs written by `make test-go`.
/.test-logs/

# Full verifier output kept by `node infra/verify/run-all.mjs`. Kept on disk on
# purpose: the tool it replaced scraped summary lines and threw the output away,
# so a failure that did not repeat could not be looked at afterwards.
/.verify-logs/

# The design mockups and their media, local-only in full.
#
# WHY THE WHOLE FOLDER AND NOT JUST THE MEDIA. The narrower rules that used to
# sit here kept 20 MB of video and photographs out while leaving seven mockup
# HTML files and a logo PNG in. The owner's decision on 2026-09-21 is that the
# design source stays on the design machine: it is an input to the frontend,
# not part of it, and the frontend it specifies is what ships.
#
# WHAT THIS COSTS, so nobody rediscovers it as a bug: the outputs of
# services/web/scripts/make-logo-assets.mjs ARE committed
# (public/arcana-mark.png, public/brand/*), but its INPUT is
# `web example/Assets/arcana logo.png` and now is not. The script refuses with
# a sentence saying so rather than an ENOENT stack. Regenerating the brand
# assets needs the design machine.
#
# Git history is permanent — these files remain in every clone made before
# this commit, and `git rm` does not shrink them. This stops new ones.
#
# Per-machine editor and tool directories are NOT listed here. They are one
# person's setup, not a project decision, so they belong in that person's
# .git/info/exclude — which is local and never committed. A shared ignore file
# that names one contributor's tooling is a decision made on everybody's behalf.
/web example/
